From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomas Mihalicka <tomas@mihalicka.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 16:48:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 04/10] Serve payments over HTTP

Expose the service to tenants. POST /v1/tenants/{tenant}/payments takes
an amount and currency plus an Idempotency-Key header, charges the
payment through the provider and returns it, and
GET /v1/tenants/{tenant}/payments/{id} looks one up. Every lookup is
scoped to the tenant in the path, so a tenant can never read another
tenant's payment.

Repeating a request with the same idempotency key returns the original
payment instead of charging again. If the payment's outcome is still
unknown, it is charged again. Reusing a key for a different amount is
a conflict.

The payment is saved before it is charged, so a crash mid-charge
leaves a record for the client's retry to pick up. The store refuses a
second payment under a key that is already taken, so two concurrent
requests with one key cannot both create a payment.

The binary reads its listen address and data directory from the
environment and shuts down gracefully on Ctrl+C.

Areas: domain, infrastructure
